1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
And now, to you, Scoutmasters.

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:10,280
Hey, welcome back to the Scoutmasters podcast.

3
00:00:10,740 --> 00:00:11,740
Mark Twain said,

4
00:00:11,880 --> 00:00:16,260
When I was a boy of 14, my father was so ignorant,

5
00:00:17,140 --> 00:00:19,960
I could hardly stand to have the old man around.

6
00:00:20,240 --> 00:00:23,440
But, but, when I got to be 21,

7
00:00:23,440 --> 00:00:27,100
I was astonished at how much the old man had learned in seven years.

8
00:00:27,100 --> 00:00:32,940
If you're living in the same house with a boy of 14,

9
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:34,800
you know exactly what I'm talking about.

10
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:38,360
And I've been there, but I got to tell you,

11
00:00:38,780 --> 00:00:40,280
they do turn around after a while.

12
00:00:41,080 --> 00:00:41,260
Yeah.

13
00:00:41,960 --> 00:00:44,880
They go from thinking they're much smarter than we are,

14
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:46,880
they can't stand to be around you,

15
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:49,300
to whether I actually talk to you like a human being.

16
00:00:50,440 --> 00:00:52,700
Hang on, it'll, it'll, it'll change.

17
00:00:52,940 --> 00:00:54,180
It will, trust me.

18
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:58,140
This is podcast number 11.

19
00:01:04,140 --> 00:01:04,560
Hey!

20
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:06,660
Hey!

21
00:01:06,660 --> 00:01:07,060
Hey!

22
00:01:07,060 --> 00:01:17,400
Hey!

23
00:01:17,400 --> 00:01:37,960
If you're a blogger or a podcaster, you already know what I'm going to talk about here.

24
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:42,600
What happens is, is you sit down somewhere and you set up your microphone and your computer

25
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:48,220
and you yap and then you try and fix things and you work on stuff

26
00:01:48,220 --> 00:01:51,880
and you try and put together something that you hope is going to help people.

27
00:01:52,539 --> 00:01:54,039
And you have no idea.

28
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:57,820
You have no idea what's going on once it's published.

29
00:01:58,380 --> 00:02:02,699
If you're very fortunate, you get a little bit of feedback here and there

30
00:02:02,699 --> 00:02:10,400
and people start sending you comments and questions and reviews of the work that you've been doing.

31
00:02:10,400 --> 00:02:13,340
And it's very satisfying.

32
00:02:14,880 --> 00:02:18,720
One of my listeners sent me this audio feedback,

33
00:02:19,060 --> 00:02:22,240
and it's always nice to get a little bit of a pat on the back,

34
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:27,700
especially when you're just sitting in a room somewhere talking into a microphone.

35
00:02:28,420 --> 00:02:29,800
So, hey, here it is.

36
00:02:30,120 --> 00:02:31,500
Hey, Clark. This is Kevin.

37
00:02:32,260 --> 00:02:34,060
I thought I'd give you a little audio feedback.

38
00:02:34,060 --> 00:02:37,100
I just listened to podcast number nine,

39
00:02:37,380 --> 00:02:43,800
and I just wanted to say thank you for imparting with all of the wisdom that you've gathered over the years in scouting

40
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:48,620
and making it available to us green scouters, if you will.

41
00:02:48,940 --> 00:02:52,780
This is my first year as a scouter, and I'm loving it,

42
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:55,120
and I hope I can put in as much time as you have.

43
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:58,240
So, again, thanks a lot for all you do, and thanks for sharing,

44
00:02:58,440 --> 00:02:59,660
and I'll talk to you again soon.

45
00:02:59,980 --> 00:03:01,060
Take care. See you.

46
00:03:01,060 --> 00:03:06,100
Kevin was kind enough to email me earlier about a couple of the earlier podcasts.

47
00:03:06,320 --> 00:03:07,480
I'm no audio engineer.

48
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:11,500
If you haven't been able to figure that out, I really have no idea.

49
00:03:11,820 --> 00:03:13,300
I'm learning as I go along.

50
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:18,080
And what I had been doing was I had messed up some of the levels in recording

51
00:03:18,080 --> 00:03:22,640
so that some parts of the podcast were quieter and some parts were really loud,

52
00:03:22,700 --> 00:03:25,960
and apparently I was driving Kevin out of his pickup truck cab

53
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:30,140
because he would have the volume turned up and something would blast at him,

54
00:03:30,220 --> 00:03:32,980
and then he'd have to turn it down, and then he would have to turn it up again.

55
00:03:33,860 --> 00:03:38,620
But Kevin was kind enough to point that out to me and recommend a couple of solutions,

56
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:45,220
and I hope the past few podcasts have shown that his expertise has gone to work.

57
00:03:45,220 --> 00:03:52,060
So right back at you, Kevin, I have to thank you for helping me out and helping me improve the podcast.

58
00:03:52,480 --> 00:03:55,579
Remember, you can get in touch with me in a variety of ways.

59
00:03:55,780 --> 00:04:00,640
The blog is scoutmaster.typepad.com, and you can leave a comment there.

60
00:04:00,640 --> 00:04:09,799
You can email me at ClarkGreen, C-L-A-R-K-E-G-R-E-E-N, all one word, at gmail.com.

61
00:04:10,579 --> 00:04:15,320
And I'm also on Facebook at Scoutmaster Blog and on Twitter at Scoutmaster Blog.

62
00:04:15,760 --> 00:04:17,880
Thanks very much for the feedback again, Kevin.

63
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:21,360
Thanks for all of you who have been emailing and leaving comments.

64
00:04:21,620 --> 00:04:22,800
Certainly do appreciate it.

65
00:04:22,900 --> 00:04:26,800
So this time around we're going to talk in Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

66
00:04:26,800 --> 00:04:30,960
I'm going to give you a little report on an outing that my troop recently took.

67
00:04:31,080 --> 00:04:38,480
It might give you a program idea, but the real point is to kind of explore the youth leadership role in a troop

68
00:04:38,480 --> 00:04:43,020
and how it exactly works, and I have a little interview with my senior patrol leader as a part of it.

69
00:04:43,720 --> 00:04:49,240
Then I have a story to tell you about one of my most memorable nights at summer camp,

70
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:57,100
and then we're going to start with number one of a four-part feature about avoiding the drama

71
00:04:57,100 --> 00:05:01,560
that can sometimes surround all the stuff that needs to get done to become an Eagle Scout,

72
00:05:01,760 --> 00:05:04,660
and we can maybe avoid a little bit of the drama, huh?

73
00:05:04,980 --> 00:05:06,160
Well, let's get started.

74
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:12,780
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

75
00:05:19,240 --> 00:05:25,520
I interviewed our senior patrol leader on a recent outing, and I had two intentions.

76
00:05:25,720 --> 00:05:33,340
One was to kind of give him the opportunity to talk about his role in setting up the outing

77
00:05:33,340 --> 00:05:38,680
and leading the Scouts, and also to give you a program idea,

78
00:05:39,140 --> 00:05:43,300
which is an outing that we have done now for the past three or four years.

79
00:05:43,420 --> 00:05:48,880
The Scouts have elected to put it on the schedule, and it's called the Magical Mystery Tour.

80
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:54,000
So the mystery part of it is none of the Scouts, except maybe the senior patrol leader

81
00:05:54,000 --> 00:05:56,840
and maybe one or two other Scouts who are working on the program,

82
00:05:57,500 --> 00:06:01,000
actually know what is going to happen on the outing.

83
00:06:01,680 --> 00:06:07,400
And what they have done is over time this has developed into an outing

84
00:06:07,400 --> 00:06:11,220
where we explore some world culture.

85
00:06:11,220 --> 00:06:14,800
One of the times we did this, it was Mongolia.

86
00:06:14,800 --> 00:06:22,060
One of the times it was the Inuit cultures, which are, I guess, most people would know as Eskimos,

87
00:06:22,280 --> 00:06:26,020
but the Inuit people who live in the northern polar regions.

88
00:06:26,780 --> 00:06:28,780
One of the times it was Scotland.

89
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:32,900
And this most recent one was Australia.

90
00:06:33,740 --> 00:06:35,200
And it's interesting.

91
00:06:35,320 --> 00:06:38,760
The guys build a program around the theme.

92
00:06:38,760 --> 00:06:45,080
And I'm going to get you started by listening to some of this interview I did with the senior patrol leader.

93
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:54,940
Prior to the trip, since this trip is a special trip in which we don't necessarily let the Scouts know exactly what we're going to do,

94
00:06:55,039 --> 00:07:00,240
so we've just done basic camping skills to make sure that they're actually ready for winter camping,

95
00:07:00,240 --> 00:07:06,660
I went out and researched about the Aborigines culture.

96
00:07:07,300 --> 00:07:13,420
And I was also in charge of getting food for tonight's dinner, which I can't say because they still don't know what it is.

97
00:07:14,360 --> 00:07:16,820
And that was a lot of fun.

98
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:19,340
And so I had to research some stuff for that.

99
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:28,940
And I had to make sure that when we left the church to come here that all the gear was in place

100
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:34,640
and everybody had a ride and permission slip and stuff and double check on that.

101
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:38,820
So here I'm just going to interrupt and mention a couple of things.

102
00:07:38,820 --> 00:07:43,539
The secret dinner he's talking about was kangaroo burgers.

103
00:07:43,540 --> 00:07:50,860
And I was really amazed at his ability to go out and find the fixings for kangaroo burgers.

104
00:07:51,200 --> 00:07:52,300
He did a really good job.

105
00:07:52,580 --> 00:07:53,800
Actual kangaroo meat.

106
00:07:54,600 --> 00:07:55,880
So, hey, who knew?

107
00:07:56,400 --> 00:07:57,700
Here, I'll let him continue.

108
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:02,720
The adult leaders are there for support for the youth scouts.

109
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:04,660
Make sure that things run smoothly.

110
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:09,000
They're there to help out the scouts whenever there's a problem.

111
00:08:09,920 --> 00:08:12,320
And they help us come up with a solution.

112
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:15,060
They also overwatch.

113
00:08:15,540 --> 00:08:23,380
And when needed, they can also help out with stations or ideas as to what we can do

114
00:08:23,380 --> 00:08:25,920
and sort of guide the scouts.

115
00:08:26,680 --> 00:08:27,960
Most trips are like this.

116
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:32,960
One person in leadership doesn't always have to be the senior patrol leader.

117
00:08:33,059 --> 00:08:34,100
Normally it's not, actually.

118
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:38,600
Another youth member would be in charge of planning it, setting it up,

119
00:08:38,600 --> 00:08:42,240
and developing a program for that weekend.

120
00:08:42,320 --> 00:08:44,540
Troop meetings.

121
00:08:44,980 --> 00:08:46,580
We have two troop meetings a month.

122
00:08:46,940 --> 00:08:51,680
We also have a patrol meetings council and a patrol meeting.

123
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:55,500
The troop meetings are normally the two meetings before a trip.

124
00:08:56,040 --> 00:08:57,100
We do demonstrations.

125
00:08:57,740 --> 00:08:58,380
We instruct.

126
00:08:58,380 --> 00:09:02,660
And we have the patrols go so that they can learn.

127
00:09:03,000 --> 00:09:04,520
And we do that for two Mondays.

128
00:09:05,200 --> 00:09:12,560
In those meetings, we also finish any gear checks we need to do.

129
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:22,119
And if anybody needs some minor advancements, we can probably get some minor advancement work required done.

130
00:09:22,339 --> 00:09:23,660
It's actually really easy.

131
00:09:24,040 --> 00:09:30,540
We do all the plannings at patrol meeting councils, which are the Monday after a trip.

132
00:09:30,540 --> 00:09:41,699
That's where all the youth leadership get together and all the patrol leaders get together, along with the adults, just to advise and put in their input if we ask.

133
00:09:41,700 --> 00:09:53,080
What we do there is we'll set up the meetings coming up and we'll set up trip ideas coming up and who's going to be running those trips so that things run smoothly.

134
00:09:53,200 --> 00:10:01,220
And we try to do these a couple, at least a couple of months and ahead so that we know what we're going to do.

135
00:10:01,660 --> 00:10:03,100
And it stays pretty organized.

136
00:10:03,520 --> 00:10:04,840
It's actually going really nicely.

137
00:10:04,840 --> 00:10:14,380
We got to build our didgeridoos and paint them last night, along with we played our Australian ruled football, which was a lot of fun.

138
00:10:15,240 --> 00:10:17,640
The weather is kind of cold, but we're getting through.

139
00:10:18,160 --> 00:10:19,520
And it's going really great.

140
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:26,700
We have dinner coming up, and it should be a really fun rest of the trip.

141
00:10:28,320 --> 00:10:34,220
So if you're not too sure about a didgeridoo or what a didgeridoo is, I'm really not going to try to explain it here.

142
00:10:34,220 --> 00:10:36,580
But it is an aboriginal instrument.

143
00:10:37,280 --> 00:10:46,180
And here's an example of what they sound like, at least just after you've made them and you try to play them.

144
00:11:04,220 --> 00:11:22,360
The important takeaways from this are the independence that they had in making decisions and in figuring out what they were going to do.

145
00:11:22,360 --> 00:11:29,080
The role of adults being in a very background kind of supporting role.

146
00:11:29,320 --> 00:11:37,200
They're to answer questions, to help out, to do the things that they can't do, like make reservations and drive cars and such.

147
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:39,780
It turned out to be a very enjoyable weekend.

148
00:11:40,220 --> 00:11:41,700
The boys really liked it.

149
00:11:41,860 --> 00:11:50,080
They have added Australian rules football, or their interpretation thereof, to their slate of games that they love to play.

150
00:11:50,080 --> 00:12:01,920
And I hope this kind of helps fill out the picture of a youth-led operation and also gives you a little bit of a program idea.

151
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:09,540
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

152
00:12:12,740 --> 00:12:16,400
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

153
00:12:16,400 --> 00:12:23,400
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

154
00:12:23,400 --> 00:12:26,780
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

155
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:30,140
You were always on the Beat Boy, Beat Boy.

156
00:12:30,280 --> 00:12:33,620
Yeah, you were always on the Beat Boy, Beat Boy.

157
00:12:33,900 --> 00:12:37,120
I'm hanging in the street, boy, street, boy.

158
00:12:37,540 --> 00:12:40,439
He was dancing to the Beat Boy, Beat Boy.

159
00:12:40,640 --> 00:12:43,860
Yeah, you're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

160
00:12:43,860 --> 00:12:47,160
You're my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

161
00:12:48,060 --> 00:12:51,480
He's my favorite all-time Boy Scout.

162
00:12:52,000 --> 00:13:00,100
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way anyone could make this up.

163
00:13:00,100 --> 00:13:11,740
Some of the best times I ever spent in scouting was the years that I worked as a staff member at our summer camp.

164
00:13:12,200 --> 00:13:13,780
It was just a wonderful time.

165
00:13:13,900 --> 00:13:21,860
And we got to see some things that, you know, a general person going down and camping and kind of hanging out would not be able to see.

166
00:13:21,860 --> 00:13:26,060
Some of these were good and some of these were bad.

167
00:13:26,300 --> 00:13:34,080
But one night stands out as being a really remarkable evening at our camp.

168
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:40,980
So around about, I guess, around about 10 o'clock, tap sounds.

169
00:13:41,160 --> 00:13:44,340
And the campers are all off to their campsites and off to bed.

170
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:48,900
And one of my friends and I decided we were going to head into town.

171
00:13:48,900 --> 00:13:51,800
And so we took the old camp road out.

172
00:13:52,199 --> 00:13:55,560
And, you know, the camp road is about a mile long or so.

173
00:13:55,720 --> 00:13:57,240
It's, you know, a dirt and gravel road.

174
00:13:57,319 --> 00:14:00,939
And as we're headed out, there's a car coming back into camp.

175
00:14:01,060 --> 00:14:07,520
And I'm looking at this car and I'm thinking, that's kind of unusual at this time of night for somebody to be headed into camp.

176
00:14:07,520 --> 00:14:09,860
And they're kind of swerving around and things like that.

177
00:14:09,860 --> 00:14:12,300
And we see this car approaching.

178
00:14:12,440 --> 00:14:15,220
So we kind of stop and we signal them to stop.

179
00:14:15,320 --> 00:14:19,040
And there's a couple of fellows in the car who we don't know.

180
00:14:19,460 --> 00:14:22,640
And we asked them if they're camped there this week.

181
00:14:22,660 --> 00:14:24,160
And they said, no, we're kind of lost.

182
00:14:24,280 --> 00:14:27,840
And we say, well, you know, you're headed into a scout camp.

183
00:14:27,900 --> 00:14:32,020
You probably want to turn around and head back out onto the main road.

184
00:14:32,600 --> 00:14:38,540
And it appeared to us as though these guys had had a few too many.

185
00:14:38,540 --> 00:14:42,500
And we're very confused about where they were headed.

186
00:14:48,280 --> 00:14:53,079
So they said, yeah, well, you know, we'll turn around, I guess.

187
00:14:53,160 --> 00:14:53,959
We'll head on out.

188
00:14:54,140 --> 00:14:56,319
And they were kind of arguing with each other.

189
00:14:56,319 --> 00:15:08,160
But I got back in our car and we proceeded to head out the road, imagining these guys would turn around and head out with us.

190
00:15:08,160 --> 00:15:12,600
And, nope, they just kept on going down into camp and we saw that.

191
00:15:13,079 --> 00:15:15,439
And so we turned around and we followed them into camp.

192
00:15:15,560 --> 00:15:16,560
And we stopped them.

193
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:18,760
And, oh, one thing led to another.

194
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:23,079
And, you know, it turned out to be a couple of guys who had a bit too much to drink.

195
00:15:23,400 --> 00:15:26,160
And we called the police.

196
00:15:26,160 --> 00:15:36,520
And so my buddy and I got back in our car and headed out to go and wait at the gate for the police to show up.

197
00:15:36,939 --> 00:15:39,280
So we were waiting there for 10 or 15 minutes.

198
00:15:39,439 --> 00:15:44,140
And we see some emergency lights up on the main road.

199
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:45,699
And we say, well, there's the police.

200
00:15:45,800 --> 00:15:46,420
They're coming in.

201
00:15:46,420 --> 00:15:48,839
And so we're sitting at the gate.

202
00:15:49,140 --> 00:15:54,180
And I stepped out into the road and kind of waved to indicate that we were there.

203
00:15:54,240 --> 00:15:56,400
We were going to lead them into camp and show them what the problem was.

204
00:15:56,760 --> 00:16:07,640
And a fire truck comes through, screaming through the gate of the camp, right past us, beeping the horns, sirens and everything,

205
00:16:07,640 --> 00:16:09,959
and starts screaming down into camp.

206
00:16:10,520 --> 00:16:11,439
A fire truck.

207
00:16:11,439 --> 00:16:11,459
A fire truck.

208
00:16:11,459 --> 00:16:11,500
A fire truck.

209
00:16:16,420 --> 00:16:22,199
So I looked at my buddy and he looked at me and we said, well, we better get back down into camp.

210
00:16:22,300 --> 00:16:22,939
Something's wrong.

211
00:16:23,640 --> 00:16:28,479
And so we got in the car and we started heading down following this fire truck.

212
00:16:28,620 --> 00:16:30,240
And we get a certain way down the road.

213
00:16:30,240 --> 00:16:41,780
And all of a sudden there is this amazing bright light shining down from the sky into a grove of trees beside the road.

214
00:16:41,780 --> 00:16:48,500
And we were really freaked out because it looked like an alien invasion.

215
00:16:48,620 --> 00:16:49,360
I'm not kidding.

216
00:16:49,520 --> 00:16:55,800
I mean, it was this tremendously bright spotlight just going through the trees and everything.

217
00:16:56,459 --> 00:16:59,680
And, you know, we hit the gas.

218
00:17:00,199 --> 00:17:02,839
We said, oh, my gosh, what in the world is happening?

219
00:17:02,840 --> 00:17:10,620
And we started heading back down the road into camp.

220
00:17:11,180 --> 00:17:21,400
And we got into camp and we found out that what had happened is there had been a staff swim at the pool after taps.

221
00:17:21,400 --> 00:17:25,700
And one of the fellows had injured himself.

222
00:17:26,420 --> 00:17:31,180
And they got so upset about the injury, they decided they would call in the medevac unit.

223
00:17:31,760 --> 00:17:33,380
And this is what the bright light was.

224
00:17:33,440 --> 00:17:38,400
It was this helicopter with, I guess they call it a midnight sun.

225
00:17:38,480 --> 00:17:39,560
And it's properly named.

226
00:17:39,660 --> 00:17:42,720
It's this giant searchlight on the helicopter.

227
00:17:43,140 --> 00:17:45,080
Of course, they had no idea where the pool was.

